Bands ley recognises. Without --mode, tune uses the band's mode and
bandwidth; outside every band it uses NFM and says "no band recognised".
usb/lsb means the sideband follows the amateur convention: USB at and above
10 MHz, LSB below (ley help modes).

  AM broadcast    530.000 kHz to 1.700 MHz      am       10 kHz     medium-wave broadcast stations
  160 m amateur   1.800 MHz to 2.000 MHz        usb/lsb  2.8 kHz    amateur radio, LSB voice
  80 m amateur    3.500 MHz to 4.000 MHz        usb/lsb  2.8 kHz    amateur radio, LSB voice
  40 m amateur    7.000 MHz to 7.300 MHz        usb/lsb  2.8 kHz    amateur radio, LSB voice
  20 m amateur    14.000 MHz to 14.350 MHz      usb/lsb  2.8 kHz    amateur radio, USB voice
  15 m amateur    21.000 MHz to 21.450 MHz      usb/lsb  2.8 kHz    amateur radio, USB voice
  CB              26.965 MHz to 27.405 MHz      am       10 kHz     citizens band, channel 1 to 40
  10 m amateur    28.000 MHz to 29.700 MHz      usb/lsb  2.8 kHz    amateur radio, USB voice
  6 m amateur     50.000 MHz to 54.000 MHz      nfm      12.5 kHz   amateur radio, FM voice and repeaters; USB below 50.3
  FM broadcast    87.500 MHz to 108.000 MHz     wfm      200 kHz    wideband FM radio stations
  airband         118.000 MHz to 137.000 MHz    am       10 kHz     aircraft and towers, AM voice
  2 m amateur     144.000 MHz to 148.000 MHz    nfm      12.5 kHz   amateur radio, FM voice and repeaters
  MURS 151 MHz    151.810 MHz to 151.950 MHz    nfm      11.25 kHz  MURS channels 1 to 3, licence-free, 11.25 kHz wide
  MURS 154 MHz    154.560 MHz to 154.610 MHz    nfm      20 kHz     MURS channels 4 and 5, licence-free, 20 kHz wide
  marine VHF      156.000 MHz to 162.025 MHz    nfm      12.5 kHz   ship and coast stations; channel 16 is 156.800
  NOAA weather    162.400 MHz to 162.550 MHz    nfm      12.5 kHz   continuous weather broadcasts, WX1 to WX7
  1.25 m amateur  222.000 MHz to 225.000 MHz    nfm      12.5 kHz   amateur radio, FM voice and repeaters
  70 cm amateur   420.000 MHz to 450.000 MHz    nfm      12.5 kHz   amateur radio, FM voice and repeaters
  GMRS 462 MHz    462.5375 MHz to 462.7375 MHz  nfm      20 kHz     GMRS/FRS channels 1 to 7 and 15 to 22: simplex and the repeater outputs -- scan here to find a repeater's transmit
  GMRS 467 MHz    467.5375 MHz to 467.7375 MHz  nfm      20 kHz     GMRS/FRS channels 8 to 14 (low power) and the repeater inputs, the uplink a radio transmits to a repeater
  GMRS            462.5375 MHz to 467.7375 MHz  nfm      20 kHz     GMRS/FRS, the whole service: both halves 5 MHz apart; scan sweeps it, a picture or a watch takes gmrs-462 or gmrs-467
  MURS            151.810 MHz to 154.610 MHz    nfm      11.25 kHz  MURS, the whole service: three channels near 151.9 MHz and two near 154.6 MHz; scan sweeps it, a picture or a watch takes murs-151 or murs-154

  ley tune noaa                noaa is an alias of wx1, 162.550 MHz
  ley tune calling             146.520 MHz, 2 m simplex calling
  ley tune guard               121.500 MHz, AM
  ley tune 16 --band marine    marine channel 16 by the number its radios print
